    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/isprs-archives-xlviii-m-11-2026-1-2026",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Climate change is driving significant cryospheric degradation in the European Alps, potentially increasing the frequency of glacial hazards such as ice avalanches. This study presents an integrated, reproducible workflow to assess the vulnerability of hydroelectric infrastructure in the Aosta Valley, Italy. The methodology combines a statistical analysis of historical European events with stochastic numerical modelling. A robust relationship between collapse volume and the angle of reach was derived using bootstrap regression. Glacier release points were identified by intersecting flowlines with glacier fronts, applying a densification strategy for complex ice bodies. Potential runout scenarios were simulated using the r.randomwalk model on a normalized 24 m Digital Elevation Model (DEM), comparing datasets from 2000 and 2008 to evaluate sensitivity to glacial retreat. Results highlight localized criticalities, particularly in the Valpelline sector, where collapses from the Luseney and Solatset glaciers could directly impact intake structures and reservoirs. In Valtournenche, simulations identify scenarios that approach the Perr\u00e8res power plant. Despite these specific risks, the regional energy network demonstrates high overall resilience. This approach provides a valuable tool for prioritizing monitoring and mitigation strategies in high-mountain energy planning.",
    "field": "Glacial hazard risk assessment",
    "problem": "The increasing frequency of climate-driven ice avalanches poses a growing threat to high-altitude infrastructure, yet a standardized, reproducible workflow for systematically assessing this risk by integrating historical event data with numerical simulations is lacking.",
    "method": "The authors propose an integrated workflow using statistical analysis of historical events and stochastic numerical modelling to assess the vulnerability of hydroelectric infrastructure to potential glacier ice avalanches.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and apply an integrated, reproducible workflow to assess the risk of glacier ice avalanches to hydroelectric infrastructure in the Aosta Valley, Italy, by combining historical event analysis with stochastic numerical modelling.",
